{
  "dom_id": "step-q72294",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The point $P$ with coordinates $(0,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(x-1)-2?$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=f(x-1)-2$ is a translation of the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $1$ unit to the *right* and $2$ units *down*. So the $y$-coordinate decreases by $2$ and the $x$-coordinate increases by $1.$\n\nWe know that the point $(0,3)$ lies on $y=f(x).$ Therefore, the point $(0+1,3-2)=(1,1)$ must lie on $y=f(x-1)-2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14214993/assets/64d0dc944682b12702eb8a2fa9de0ef3e65cc57ac1cc7a242a09c986ca7f955c.png)",
